// A NARROWING transfer must not truncate in silence.
//
// Design 170 made every narrowing conversion written: `x as UInt8` panics out
// of range, `UInt8.from(x)` answers `None`, `UInt8.from(truncating: x)` is the
// deliberate wrap. A transfer through a platform `Int` bypasses all three:
//
//   let n: Int = 300
//   let b: Int8 = n          // prints 44
//
// `_types_compatible` admits a platform `Int`/`UInt` into any integer type, a
// permission that exists so a bare LITERAL can adopt a fixed-width slot
// (`let x: Int8 = 42`). Design 87's expected-type propagation does that job
// properly now, so what the permission actually covers today is a runtime
// value silently losing its high bits. Between two FIXED widths the same
// transfer is already a clean error, so the hole is exactly the platform pair.
//
// Design 195 left this open deliberately: it is a behavioral flip with a
// consumer sweep of its own, and it belongs with design 170's conversion rules
// rather than with 195's operand-agreement rule.
// XFAIL: DF-195b a narrowing transfer through a platform `Int` truncates silently instead of being written
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: Int8

func main() {
    let n: Int = 300
    let b: Int8 = n
    print(b)
}
